//
// loft#2035 — A SPATIAL SLICE IS NOT A VALUE (`@FR-Slice-KeyedIter`, `@FR-Slice-Box`).  Every
// keyed range slice is a `for`-only iterator and a value position is a STATIC ERROR; sorted,
// index and trie refused it, and spatial — the rule's third kind — compiled `b = s[(0,0)..(2,2)]`
// and answered one bogus record (len 1, sum 1 over a box holding 10 + 20), on both backends.
// The sibling file iterates the same three forms, which is the half that must keep working.
//
// @falsified-at: 5dcb0afa3 — hand-measured, interpret and native: the control COMPILES this file
//   and prints `len 1 sum 1`, so the expected refusal is missing on both backends.
// CHANNEL: the expected diagnostic.
// ARMED: no instrument — the refusal is on by default.
// HOLDS: the iterated forms (a-keyed-range-walks-its-key-interval-in-declared-order.loft).
// @EXPECT_ERROR: a spatial slice is a `for`-loop iterator, not a value

struct P { x: integer, y: integer, v: integer }

fn main() {
  s: spatial<P[x, y]> = [P { x: 1, y: 1, v: 10 }, P { x: 5, y: 5, v: 50 }, P { x: 2, y: 0, v: 20 }];
  b = s[(0, 0)..(2, 2)];
  t = 0;
  for q in b { t += q.v; }
  println("len {len(b)} sum {t}");
}
